networkreporting 0.3.2
========================
* `true_visibility_from_network()` and `visibility_accuracy()`, for checking a
  visibility rule against a known network.

  Where the whole reporting network is known -- so, in practice, simulation --
  every alter's real visibility is known too, and a rule can be asked not
  "which tier fired" but "was it right". That is the only way to catch a rule
  that returns finite, plausible, wrong numbers, which is the failure this whole
  layer exists to prevent.

  The definition is short enough that it kept being written inline, and kept
  being written differently. Two things go wrong and neither announces itself,
  so both are now settled in one place:

    - WHOSE frame membership counts. Visibility is the number of frame-population
      members who could report an alter, so it is the REPORTER's frame status
      that matters, never the alter's. Filtering on the alter's yields a
      different quantity that looks just as reasonable.
    - DUPLICATE ties. A census linking a pair by more than one route carries it
      more than once, and counting rows then doubles the answer. In one socsim
      census this made an exact rule look 27% wrong.

  `visibility_accuracy()` scores predictions separately for on-frame and
  off-frame alters and reports the ratio between the two errors. That ratio is
  the number to read: every death is off-frame while exposure is a mixture, so
  an error of the same size on both sides largely cancels out of a rate and a
  differential one biases it. An overall accuracy figure is close to useless
  here.

  Checked against the socsim Bangladesh networks: the canonical definition
  reproduces the hand-rolled truth exactly on 30,193 sibling alters and 49,886
  cousin-union alters, and the scoring reproduces the published differentials of
  1.000 and 1.026.

* estimands that are not ratios. A death rate is a ratio of two
  visibility-adjusted sums, and those sums -- the estimated number of events and
  the estimated person-time -- are estimands in their own right.

  What was missing was uncertainty on them. The bootstrap already computed
  replicates of `num.hat` and `denom.hat`, and the summarising step then
  discarded everything but the ratio, so a rate was the only thing the estimator
  could report with an interval. Both sums now get their own CI, standard error
  and median, and `estimated_total()` reads one out.

  The rate's own interval is unchanged, which is what makes this safe: the
  change adds columns and moves no number. Forming a rate by hand from the two
  totals reproduces `asdr.hat` exactly, which is the separation of ratio
  formation from sum formation the plan asked for.

  Worth knowing, and documented on `estimated_total()`: a sum is a *population*
  total only if the survey weights are population weights. With relative weights
  it is off by a constant factor -- silently, since nothing in the data says
  which kind was used. The rate is unaffected either way because the factor
  cancels, which is exactly why the distinction can go unnoticed until somebody
  asks for a total.

* `tie_cell_vars()` decides which columns define a cell by matching the shape of
  a derived column rather than enumerating the estimator's outputs. The
  enumeration was brittle: adding intervals for `num.hat` and `denom.hat`
  immediately turned them into cell variables and broke every tie comparison.

* combining estimates across ties: `compare_ties()`, `pool_ties()` and
  `ties_union_check()`.

  The plan listed compare, union and pool as three variants of one operation.
  They are not. `compare_ties()` and `pool_ties()` combine RESULTS; union
  combines DATA -- it pools the reports and re-estimates -- so it cannot be done
  from finished estimates at all, and the three do not share an interface.

  `compare_ties()` lines the ties up on shared cells and assumes nothing. Where
  two ties disagree by more than their intervals allow, that is information
  about how they differ, and averaging it away before looking at it would be a
  mistake.

  `pool_ties()` averages them. The thing it has to get right is that the ties
  are NOT independent: a multi-tie survey asks the same respondents about every
  tie, so a respondent weighted up perturbs all of them at once. Inverse-variance
  pooling assumes that away. Where the ties were bootstrapped with the same
  replicate weights, pooling within each replicate measures the correlation
  instead -- and that is the default. `method = "analytic"` is available and
  warns. On the package's own test fixture the two intervals differ by about a
  quarter, in a direction that is a property of the data rather than something
  that can be reasoned out beforehand.

  `ties_union_check()` reports whether a union is possible rather than
  performing one. Union needs an identity for alters that holds ACROSS ties, and
  alter ids here are unique only within an ego; given a key it measures the
  overlap and says what the union would involve. It also flags the part that is
  easy to miss: the union of two ties is generally not the structure of either,
  so it needs its own `tie_config()`. Two cliques unioned are usually not a
  clique.

* `vis_from_report()` -- visibility as the respondent reported it. Ego is asked,
  about each alter, how many frame-population members that alter is connected
  to, and the answer is used directly.

  Every other rule recovers visibility from something the survey collected for
  another purpose: the tie's structure, or the respondents' own group sizes. For
  an `"unbounded"` tie there is no way round that -- neighbours and
  acquaintances have no bounded group to count, and no reason to think
  respondents resemble their alters -- so asking is the only honest route, and
  this is the rule that turns the answer into an estimate.

  Two arguments encode what the question actually asked, which the numbers
  cannot reveal: `counts.ego` (did the respondent count themselves?) and
  `counts.self` (did the answer count the alter?). With `counts.self = TRUE` it
  agrees with `vis_from_group_size()`, which is the clearer constructor for that
  case.

  A reported visibility below one is an error rather than a small number: it
  contradicts the report it sits on, since the alter was named by somebody. The
  message names the usual causes -- a don't-know code stored as 0, or
  `counts.ego` set wrongly -- and `on_impossible` offers `"floor"` and `"na"`.
  Item non-response is treated as normal, with a message pointing at
  `vis_coalesce()` rather than implying the data is broken.

* `vis_from_model()` -- visibility predicted by a fitted model rather than a
  cell mean. The third member of the predict-from-data family, and the one the
  fit/predict split was designed to accommodate: it needed a constructor and
  nothing else, no interface change anywhere.

  A cell mean is a model with one categorical predictor and no pooling. This
  adds continuous predictors, several covariates without the cell counts
  collapsing, and borrowing strength across cells rather than treating each in
  isolation.

  The formula is one-sided and written in the alter's column names -- the
  response is always the donor's own group size -- with `predictors` mapping
  names where the donor frame spells one differently, exactly as `match_on`
  does.

  Two things worth expecting. Bootstrapping it takes the expensive path, since a
  model with continuous predictors is not constant within a cell; the estimator
  warns and refits per replicate. And a predicted group size at or below zero is
  an error rather than a very small weight, since the alter was reported by
  somebody: the message points at a log link, which cannot predict one.

* **bug fix in the bootstrap for an estimated visibility rule.** The per-replicate
  refit returns one group size per row of `ec_dat`, but it was being indexed by
  bootstrap-weight row position instead, so every cell was handed the same few
  rows' values.

  This was invisible whenever the estimated visibility happened to be constant --
  a `vis_from_donor(match_on = NULL)` rule, which is what the existing tests
  used. It was wrong as soon as visibility varied by cell, which is the *common*
  case: matching on alter sex or age group means matching on the cells
  themselves. In a two-cell example the affected cell's denominator was out by a
  factor of nearly three.

  Estimates from `vis_from_clique()` are unaffected: it is not estimated, so it
  never took this path. That covers every DHS and MICS figure the packages
  produce.

* the third bootstrap case is implemented, completing the design in
  `dev/VISIBILITY-PLAN.md`. An estimated rule whose visibility is *not* constant
  within a cell -- one matching on a covariate that cuts across cells, or a
  future model rule with continuous predictors -- cannot use the frame-split
  identity. It is now refit and re-predicted for every report inside each
  replicate, with a warning naming the cost. `vis_is_cell_constant()` decides
  which path applies, and there is deliberately no silent fallback to the cheap
  one: that would freeze a sample quantity and reinstate the bug `is_estimated`
  exists to prevent.

  Where both paths are valid they agree exactly, which is asserted as a test.

* `vis_from_donor()` declares its `match_on` columns in `requires`. Without that
  the estimator did not carry them onto the report rows, so matching on anything
  that was not already a cell variable failed at the point estimate.

* `network_survival_estimator()` -- the generic entry point. Given reports about
  alters connected to respondents by some tie, it produces age-specific rates
  under both the individual- and aggregate-visibility estimators.

  `siblingsurvival::sibling_estimator()` is now a thin wrapper over it, keeping
  its exact signature and output, so there is one implementation rather than two
  that can drift. The estimator was never sibling-specific in anything but
  naming: nothing in the pipeline assumes anything about siblings, only about a
  tie.

  It has NO default `tie`, and refuses to run without one, for the same reason
  `tie_config()` has no default structure. `sibling_estimator()` supplies the
  clique tie, which is correct there.

  Output columns are generic: `alter.age` where the sibling wrapper returns
  `sib.age`.

* the estimator carries whatever columns a visibility rule declares in
  `requires` through its covariate join. Without this `vis_from_group_size()`
  was usable through `apply_visibility_rule()` but not through the estimator,
  because the join dropped the group-size column.

* `vis_from_group_size()` -- visibility from a group size the CALLER supplies,
  rather than one derived by counting ego's roster.

  Sometimes the group whose size sets an alter's visibility is not the roster
  the alter was reported on, and only the caller can say what it is. Cousins
  are reported through the maternal and paternal sides separately, but an
  alter's visibility depends on everyone who could have reported them -- both
  sides, plus ego's own siblings. "Pooled cousins excluding ego's siblings" is
  a difference of two counts, which no roster holds at all. A parent's
  visibility is the number of their children on the frame, a fact about the
  sibship rather than the parent roster. None of those can be derived; all
  three are now expressible.

  The rule still does the frame split for you -- an alter in the frame
  population cannot report themselves -- since that asymmetry is the only route
  by which visibility reaches a rate. `subtract.self = FALSE` turns it off for
  a basis that deliberately omits it, and says so in the assumptions.

  It makes no structural assumption, so it needs no `tie` and accepts any. That
  is not because it is safe the way `vis_from_donor()` is safe: it is because
  the caller has taken on the part `vis_from_clique()` would have derived. The
  package checks the arithmetic; it cannot check that the column counts the
  right people, and the assumptions say so.

  Checked against socsim ground truth on the Bangladesh reporting networks:
  given the group `vis_from_clique()` would itself derive, the two agree
  exactly, on both sides of the frame split. On a pooled cousinship the
  differential error against ground truth is 1.056, against 1.865 for the
  global donor rule the tie gate currently falls through to.

* `tie_config()` gains `ego.in.group` and `frame.indicator`. Both are facts
  about the tie rather than about the rule, so this is where they belong. Both
  default to NULL, meaning "not declared", and a tie that declares neither
  behaves exactly as before.

  Where a tie declaration meets a value set on the rule or passed to
  `apply_visibility_rule()`, agreement is fine and **disagreement is an error
  naming both sources**. There is deliberately no silent precedence: quietly
  preferring one would produce a number computed under an assumption the caller
  did not know was in force, which is the failure this layer exists to prevent.

  Note that `ego.in.group` was already in two places that did not talk to each
  other -- on `vis_from_clique()`, governing what the rule does, and on
  `apply_visibility_rule()`, governing how `y.F` is derived. They could disagree
  and nothing said so. That is now reconciled.

  A rule's assumptions are computed from the resolved state rather than fixed at
  construction, so provenance can no longer report "ego is a member of the group
  ego reports about" for a tie that declared the opposite. The resolved
  `ego.in.group` and a non-default `frame.indicator` are both reported.

  Not enforced, deliberately: that `structure` and `ego.in.group` agree. A
  household roster that excludes the respondent is a clique the respondent is
  outside of, and rosters that carry the respondent as a row count `yprime.F`
  rather than `y.F`. Both are real; the package lets them be stated.

* a visibility rule that assumes a tie structure now REFUSES to run until the
  caller declares one, with `tie_config()`. There is no default and no
  inference.

  `vis_from_clique()` computes `y.F` on frame and `y.F + 1` off it. That is a
  theorem about cliques, and given a roster of reports nothing distinguishes a
  clique from a tie that is not one: the rule returns a finite, plausible number
  either way. Applied where it does not hold it was therefore wrong with no
  outward sign, and the provenance table said `clique: 100%`, which reads as
  "the exact rule was used".

  Measured against socsim ground truth on the Bangladesh reporting networks:
  exact for 100% of sibling alters, on both sides of the frame split. On
  maternal cousins -- not a clique, since cousinship is not transitive -- it
  overstates visibility by 1.089x off-frame against 1.061x on-frame. Because every
  death is off-frame while exposure is a mixture, that 1.026x *differential* does
  not cancel out of a death rate; it biases it. The paternal cousin network
  agrees at 1.22x, so it is structural, not noise.

  `tie_config()` takes one of `"clique"` (siblings, household members),
  `"group"` (cousins), `"star"` (parents) or `"unbounded"` (neighbours,
  acquaintances).

* `vis_coalesce()` now falls through for a non-clique tie, which it previously
  could not. A tier that does not apply to the declared tie is **dropped**,
  rather than being skipped when it returns `NA` -- the clique tier never
  returns `NA`, which is why the chain used to claim 100% of cousin alters. The
  dropped tiers are named in the provenance output, so an estimate that
  discarded its exact tier says so.

* `siblingsurvival::sibling_estimator()` gains a `tie` argument defaulting to
  `tie_config("clique", name = "siblings")`. Siblings are a clique, so the
  default changes nothing and no existing estimate moves. Set it when using that
  function for a tie that is not a clique, as the socsim work does for maternal
  cousins and cousins-plus-siblings.

* visibility is now a declared, composable rule rather than a hardcoded
  assumption. A rule is a `fit`/`predict` pair, and three are supplied:

    - `vis_from_clique()` -- the exact rule, and the default everywhere. `y.F`
      for an on-frame alter, `y.F + 1` otherwise. It reproduces the previous
      behaviour bit for bit, so no existing estimate moves.
    - `vis_from_donor()` -- borrow visibility from a donor population, matched
      on covariates. This is the approximation that any tie which is not a
      clique needs, and it is the first rule whose fit consumes the sample.
    - `vis_coalesce()` -- try rules in priority order, recording which tier
      resolved each alter.

  `apply_visibility_rule()` applies a rule and returns a `vis_provenance`
  object alongside the values: which rule resolved how many alters, and what
  share of the *deaths* and of the *exposure* were approximated. Those two are
  different numbers and both are reported, since an approximation touching
  little exposure but most deaths is not a small one.

  Three points that shaped the design and are worth knowing:

    - A visibility that is constant within a cell CANCELS out of a rate and
      reduces the estimator exactly to the aggregate one. Visibility survives
      into a rate only through the on-frame/off-frame asymmetry -- every death
      is off-frame, exposure is a mixture -- so `vis_from_donor()` estimates a
      group size and reconstructs the split, rather than estimating a
      visibility directly. `tests/testthat/test_visibility_rules.R` asserts
      this as executable documentation.
    - The donor default is the weighted HARMONIC mean, not the arithmetic one.
      The individual estimator averages `1/v`, so `(E[1/v])^-1` is the
      functional that makes the plug-in unbiased. `"arithmetic"` remains
      available, since it is what the historical `y.F.bar / (y.F.bar + 1)`
      adjustment factor used.
    - Donor coverage failure is routine, not exceptional: `match_on` describes
      the alter but donors are respondents, and a survey of women aged 15-49
      has no donor for an alter aged 60. `min_donors` and `on_missing` make
      that loud instead of letting NA propagate into rates.

* `get_boot_ests_matrix()` now takes `visibility` and `refit`. Visibility used
  to be frozen across bootstrap replicates unconditionally. For
  `vis_from_clique()` that is correct -- visibility is a function of ego's own
  reports, not of who was sampled -- but for any rule estimated from the sample
  it understates the variance. `is_estimated` now decides, and an estimated
  rule with no refit function warns rather than silently freezing. This is a
  no-op for the clique rule, which is what makes it safe to land.

* `get_ec_reports()` splits occurrences by frame status
  (`y.DandFcell` / `y.DandnotFcell`) alongside the exposure pair it already
  produced, and warns if any occurrence is attributed to an on-frame alter. For
  mortality that is impossible -- a dead alter is never on the frame -- so a
  non-zero value means a miscoded frame indicator, a data-quality check the
  package did not previously have. Verified zero across the Rwanda 2010 DHS and
  Zimbabwe 2019 MICS prep paths. Existing column names are unchanged.

* `get_group_info()` generalises `get_sibship_info()`: the `+ 1` is now the
  explicit argument `ego.in.group` rather than a constant. It encodes "ego is a
  member of the group ego reports about", which holds for households as well as
  siblings and fails for parents and neighbours. `get_sibship_info()` is kept
  as the sibling-named wrapper.

* `add_esc_ind_vis()` is now implemented in terms of `vis_from_clique()`, so
  that it and the rule layer cannot drift apart. It also now honours its
  `varname` argument, which it previously ignored -- a caller passing
  `varname = 'foo'` silently got a column called `ind_vis`.

* new vignette, *Approximating visibility*.

* `network.survival.estimator_()` is deprecated, superseded by the spine. It
  still runs and still returns what it always did. The deprecation message
  records the two things it does that the spine cannot yet reproduce:
  estimating from aggregate reports plus known populations rather than from an
  ego X alter roster, and `within.alter.weights`.

* the tie-agnostic network survival estimator spine moved here from
  `siblingsurvival`, which now depends on this package. New here:
  `occ.exp()`, `cell_config()` and its `make.age.groups()` /
  `make.even.age.groups()` / `make.time.periods()` helpers,
  `get_esc_reports()`, `get_ec_reports()`, `get_ind_est_from_ec()`,
  `get_agg_est_from_ec()`, `get_boot_ests_matrix()`, `get_visibility()`,
  `add_esc_ind_vis()`, `sib_ic_checks()`, `nmx_to_nqx()` and `q15_to_50()`.
  Nothing in this package's own behaviour changes; the spine arrives alongside
  the existing estimators rather than replacing any of them.

  The move is a pure relocation: no expected value in either package's test
  suite was edited, and the DHS and MICS validation harnesses in
  `siblingsurvival` reproduce every published figure unchanged.

  Only the sibling-specific parts stayed behind in `siblingsurvival` -- the DHS
  and MICS prep functions, maternal classification, and `sibling_estimator()`
  itself. `get_visibility()` and the sibship helpers are named for siblings but
  are really *clique*-specific, holding for any tie that partitions the
  population into disjoint groups containing ego. A later release turns that
  into a declared, composable visibility rule; see `dev/VISIBILITY-PLAN.md`.

* this package now contains compiled code for the first time (`src/`,
  `LinkingTo: Rcpp`), for the occurrence/exposure calculation
* `Roxygen: list(markdown = TRUE)`, matching the moved documentation. Existing
  help pages are unaffected apart from whitespace, two `*...*` spans that now
  render as emphasis, and one `*` list that now renders as a bulleted list
* documented the `boot.chunk.size` argument of `report.aggregator_()`,
  `kp.estimator_()` and `network.survival.estimator_()`
* removed a stale `R/RcppExports.R` that declared `resample_stratum()` against a
  `src/` this package did not have; that routine lives in `surveybootstrap`
* adopted testthat edition 3; `tests/test_all.r` is now `tests/testthat.R`
* replaced defunct dplyr `select_()` and `rename_()` calls (underscore SE
  interface) with `select(all_of())` and `rename()` throughout
  `report_aggregator.r`, `known_population.r`, and `scale_up.r`

networkreporting 0.3.1
========================
* fixed a bug that caused a lazy evaluation-related error for the `dropmiss` 
  param when using `kp.estimator_` 

networkreporting 0.2.99
=======================
* updated `network.survival` function so that it returns cell-specific degree estimates

networkreporting 0.2
====================
* updated `nsum.estimator` function so that it can use an estimated
  total degree that has come from some source other than the dataset being
  used for scale-up estimates
* in `add.kp`, changed the name of the argument `total.pop.size` to be `total.popn.size`

